    "en": "Zhao",
    "zh": "赵",
    "history": "From the name of the city of Zhao, in present-day Shanxi province. \n\nMu Wang, king of the Zhou dynasty (1001–947 BC), was noted for his campaigns and journeys to distant lands and for his expansion of the Chinese empire. According to legend, he once traveled to the Kunlun mountains, then west of China, to see the Queen Mother of the West. After arriving, he learned of military attacks at home, so his chariot driver, Zao Fu, obtained eight marvelous steeds which took them back at a rate of a thousand li (500 miles) a day, so that they were able to defend the capital. In recognition of his service, Mu Wang granted to the charioteer Zao Fu the city of Zhao, and his descendants subsequently adopted Zhao as their surname.\n\nThe surname reached new heights of prominence during the Song dynasty (960 - 1279 AD), as it was the surname of the Song imperial family.",
    "historyZh": "源自位于今山西省境内的赵城之名。\n\n周穆王(公元前1001年至前947年)以远征异域、开疆拓土而著称。相传他曾西行至当时位于中国以西的昆仑山,拜见西王母。到达后,他得知国内遭受军事袭击的消息,其车夫造父便寻得八匹神骏之马,日行千里(约合500英里),助他们及时返回,保卫了国都。穆王为表彰造父的功劳,将赵城赐予他,其后代后来便以赵为姓。\n\n此姓在宋朝(公元960年至1279年)达到了新的显赫高度,因其正是宋朝皇室的姓氏。",
