    "en": "Qiu",
    "zh": "邱",
    "history": "The surname Qiu can trace its roots back to Jiang Ziya (姜子牙), a military advisor at the start of the Zhou Dynasty (1046 - 256 BC) who would later found the powerful state of Qi. The capital of Qi was a place named Yingqiu (营丘), located in present-day Shandong province. Some of Jiang Ziya’s descendants thus adopted Qiu (丘) as their surname. Thousands of years later, in 1725 AD, the Yongzheng Emperor of the Qing Dynasty decreed that a naming taboo was placed on the name of Confucius, which was Kong Qiu (孔丘). As a result, many people with the surname Qiu (丘) added a part meaning “place” (阝) to their surname, forming the word “邱” (which is still pronounced Qiu).",
    "historyZh": "丘(邱)姓可追溯至姜子牙,周朝(公元前1046年至前256年)初年的一位军事谋士,他后来建立了强盛的齐国。齐国的都城名为营丘,位于今山东省境内。姜子牙的部分后代因此以“丘”为姓。千百年后,公元1725年,清朝雍正帝下诏,规定须避讳孔子之名“孔丘”。因此,许多姓“丘”之人便在姓氏中加入表示“地方”的偏旁“阝”,组成“邱”字(读音仍为qiū)。",
